════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  scenario: text
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

=== TWO RUNGS PER LEVEL: cut, then take ===
   cutWords(the city is old)
     -> the|city|is|old
   word(the|city|is|old, old)
     -> old

=== THE DRIVER SPLITS NOTHING ===
   for (const p of paragraphs.of(text))
     for (const s of sentences.of(p.value))
       words.of(s.value);

=== UP THE LADDER: the names alternate all the way ===
   start         "old"
   word          the|city|is|old
   cutWords      the city is old
   sentence      paris is a city|the city is old
   cutSentences  paris is a city / the city is old

=== HUBS: one shared name split into three ===
   paragraph()     degree 3
   sentence()      degree 5
   word()          degree 20
   cutParagraphs() degree 1
   cutSentences()  degree 3
   cutWords()      degree 5
   biggest hub in the graph: 20  "word()"

=== PER-LEVEL QUERIES, NOW TWO HOPS ===
   every word: a city is new old paris place the tokyo
   every sentence: 5 extractions

=== WHERE THE LEVELS NO LONGER SHARE A FACT ===
   a one-word sentence, cut both ways:
     cutSentences(hello)   sentence(hello,hello)   cutWords(hello)   word(hello,hello)
   two applications now, not one — the fn name says which cut's list it is.
rendered 68 nodes, 138 edges -> data.js

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  scenario: text-index
════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

=== THE CUT: three levels, all by position ===
   paragraphs: 3
   sentences:  5 (5 distinct)
   words:      21 (9 distinct)

=== FORWARD: a paragraph's sentences, in order ===
   paris is a city
   the city is old

=== BACK TO THE SOURCE: one word, up to the raw text ===
   word      "old"
   sentence  the city is old
   paragraph paris is a city / the city is old
   document  paris is a city / the city is old /  / tokyo is a city / the city is new /  / a city is a place

=== DEDUP: one node per string, keeping every producer ===
   "city" is one node, produced by 5 applications
     paris is a city
     the city is old
     tokyo is a city
     the city is new
     a city is a place
   "a" appears twice in one sentence, so that sentence is listed twice:
     paris is a city
     tokyo is a city
     a city is a place
     a city is a place

=== THE SINGLE-CHILD CASE ===
   paragraph          a city is a place
   its only sentence  a city is a place
   same node?         true
   so sentence(p,0) points back at its own input — a 2-cycle, visible in the viewer.
